(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-drill-ent"]={"title":"Lecture 5 Ear, Nose and Throat Drugs Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antibiotic is the MAINSTAY of acute otitis media therapy, given in a higher dose to overcome Streptococcus pneumoniae resistance?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Azithromycin","Cefdinir","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin is the mainstay for acute otitis media; the higher dose is what overcomes Streptococcus pneumoniae resistance. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which ORAL antibiotic is used for SEVERE or RESISTANT otitis media, or when the child had antibiotics in the previous month?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Ampicillin-sulbactam","Piperacillin-tazobactam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin-clavulanate is the step up for severe or resistant disease or recent antibiotic exposure. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which ORAL antibiotic is the STANDARD therapy for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis, because Haemophilus influenzae often makes beta-lactamase?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Penicillin V potassium","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Dicloxacillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clavulanate protects the penicillin from beta-lactamase, which Haemophilus influenzae often produces in sinusitis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone is the single-drug alternative to clindamycin plus cefixime for sinusitis in a penicillin-allergic patient?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Ofloxacin","Azithromycin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Levofloxacin is the fluoroquinolone named as the alternative for penicillin-allergic sinusitis; it has good activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin is escalated to by injection when oral therapy for otitis media fails or is not tolerated?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Cefdinir","Cephalexin","Cefixime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ceftriaxone is given by injection, either intramuscular or intravenous, for failed therapy or when oral drugs are not tolerated. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which third-generation cephalosporin is the oral choice for otitis media in a penicillin-allergic patient or after failed therapy?","choices":["Cephalexin","Cefdinir","Ceftriaxone","Cefazolin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cefdinir is the third-generation oral cephalosporin named for penicillin allergy and for failed therapy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which antibiotic's use for otitis media is limited because up to half of Streptococcus pneumoniae resists its class?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Azithromycin is a macrolide, and up to 50 percent of Streptococcus pneumoniae resists macrolides, so it is a less reliable penicillin-allergy alternative. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which penicillin can be given as a SINGLE INTRAMUSCULAR INJECTION for group A streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["Penicillin V potassium","Ampicillin","Dicloxacillin","Benzathine penicillin G"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Benzathine penicillin G is a single intramuscular injection that replaces the full oral course for group A strep pharyngitis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which first-generation cephalosporin is a penicillin-allergy alternative for group A streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["Cephalexin","Cefdinir","Ceftriaxone","Cefixime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin is the narrow first-generation cephalosporin named for penicillin-allergic group A strep pharyngitis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is paired with cefixime for sinusitis in a penicillin-allergic patient?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Clindamycin","Levofloxacin","Azithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin plus cefixime is one of the penicillin-allergy regimens for sinusitis, and clindamycin is also a pharyngitis alternative. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which ear drop component is the classic cause of ALLERGIC CONTACT SENSITIVITY (hypersensitivity)?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Ofloxacin","Neomycin","Polymyxin B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neomycin, in the Cortisporin drops, carries a chance of hypersensitivity and is the classic cause of contact allergy in ear drops. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which ear drop is NOT recommended with a RUPTURED EARDRUM or tubes in place, because of cochlear damage and hearing loss?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin/dexamethasone","Ofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Neomycin/polymyxin B/hydrocortisone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The polymyxin B in these Cortisporin drops can reach the inner ear through a ruptured eardrum or tubes, causing cochlear damage and hearing loss. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which antifungal is a CYTOCHROME P450 3A4 INHIBITOR that prolongs the corrected QT interval?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Nystatin","Terbinafine","Caspofungin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ketoconazole inhibits cytochrome P450 3A4 and prolongs the corrected QT interval (QTc). — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which antifungal can cause HEPATITIS, cirrhosis and hepatic failure along with high cholesterol and orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Nystatin","Ketoconazole","Terbinafine","Caspofungin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ketoconazole's listed adverse effects include hepatitis, abnormal liver tests, cirrhosis, hepatic failure, hyperlipidemia and orthostatic hypotension. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which antifungal binds to sterols in the fungal cell membrane, raising its permeability?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Terbinafine","Nystatin","Caspofungin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nystatin, a nonabsorbable polyene, binds sterols in the fungal cell membrane and raises its permeability. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which NONABSORBABLE antifungal treats ORAL CANDIDIASIS (thrush) in a patient on inhaled steroids, with human immunodeficiency virus infection or on chemotherapy?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Terbinafine","Caspofungin","Nystatin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nystatin oral suspension is a nonabsorbable antifungal for oral candidiasis, which is seen with inhaled steroids, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection and chemotherapy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which antifungal causes only stomach upset (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting) because it is NOT ABSORBED?","choices":["Nystatin","Ketoconazole","Terbinafine","Caspofungin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nystatin is not absorbed, so its listed side effects are limited to diarrhea, nausea, stomach pain and vomiting. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which drug is an IRREVERSIBLE, noncompetitive inhibitor of platelets?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Naproxen","Acetaminophen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelets and does not select between cyclooxygenase 1 and 2. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which drug is linked to REYE SYNDROME in a child with a viral fever such as chickenpox or influenza?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Acetaminophen","Aspirin","Naproxen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin in children with fever from a viral illness raises the incidence of Reye syndrome. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Very low doses of which drug may benefit hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (preeclampsia)?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Acetaminophen","Aspirin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is avoided in pregnancy at ordinary doses, but very low doses may benefit preeclampsia. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which drug's toxicity causes hyperventilation with alkalosis, then fever, dehydration and metabolic acidosis, then shock and coma?","choices":["Aspirin","Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Acetaminophen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin toxicity (salicylism) runs from hyperventilation and alkalosis to fever and metabolic acidosis, then shock and coma. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which drug should NOT be taken with anticoagulants, because it inhibits platelet function?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Aspirin","Guaifenesin","Dextromethorphan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients are told not to take aspirin with anticoagulants because aspirin inhibits platelet function. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which drug raises LITHIUM and methotrexate levels and weakens angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Guaifenesin","Ibuprofen","Dextromethorphan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ibuprofen cuts the kidney's clearance of lithium and methotrexate, so their levels rise, and it blunts angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which drug causes gastric or duodenal ULCERS, edema, fluid retention and acute renal failure?","choices":["Acetaminophen","Guaifenesin","Pseudoephedrine","Ibuprofen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ibuprofen's listed adverse effects are gastric or duodenal ulcers, perforation and bleeding, edema and fluid retention, and acute renal failure. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which over-the-counter pain reliever has a LONGER HALF-LIFE than ibuprofen, so it is taken less often?","choices":["Naproxen","Acetaminophen","Aspirin","Diclofenac"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Naproxen (Aleve) has a longer half-life, so it is dosed less often than ibuprofen. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which analgesic and antipyretic has NO notable side effects at therapeutic doses?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Acetaminophen","Aspirin","Naproxen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acetaminophen is very well tolerated, with no notable side effects at therapeutic doses. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which drug raises the risk of LIVER DAMAGE (chronically) when combined with alcohol?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Aspirin","Acetaminophen","Naproxen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alcohol with acetaminophen increases the risk of liver damage, especially with chronic use. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which drug is traditionally used for ACETAMINOPHEN TOXICITY and, inhaled, splits the disulfide bonds in mucus?","choices":["Guaifenesin","Dornase alfa","Hypertonic saline","N-acetylcysteine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen toxicity and, nebulized, splits disulfide bonds that link mucoproteins. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which inhaled mucus-thinning drug leaves a ROTTEN EGG smell because of its high sulfur content?","choices":["N-acetylcysteine","Guaifenesin","Dornase alfa","Hypertonic saline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled N-acetylcysteine smells of rotten eggs because of its sulfur content, and it can cause nausea, vomiting and bronchospasm. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which nebulized drug is a DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) enzyme that thins cystic fibrosis sputum?","choices":["N-acetylcysteine","Dornase alfa","Guaifenesin","Hypertonic saline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dornase alfa cleaves DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) released from degenerating neutrophils, which lowers the viscosity of cystic fibrosis sputum. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which inhaled solution improves AIRWAY HYDRATION and mucociliary clearance?","choices":["Dornase alfa","N-acetylcysteine","Hypertonic saline","Guaifenesin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled hypertonic saline improves mucus flow properties, airway hydration, mucociliary clearance and lung function. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which first-generation antihistamine is sold over the counter as a SLEEP AID?","choices":["Cetirizine","Fexofenadine","Loratadine","Doxylamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Doxylamine is the first-generation antihistamine sold over the counter as a sleep aid, because sedation is the major side effect. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which first-generation antihistamine is rated HIGH for sedative, antiemetic and anticholinergic effects alike?","choices":["Promethazine","Chlorpheniramine","Meclizine","Loratadine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Promethazine is the first-generation antihistamine rated high for sedative, antiemetic and anticholinergic effects, with the strongest antimuscarinic actions against motion sickness. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which ORAL second-generation antihistamine is rated LOW, rather than very low, for sedation?","choices":["Fexofenadine","Cetirizine","Loratadine","Chlorpheniramine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cetirizine is rated low for sedation, while the other oral second-generation agents, fexofenadine and loratadine, are rated very low. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which intranasal antihistamine causes a BITTER TASTE and nosebleeds?","choices":["Cetirizine","Fexofenadine","Azelastine","Loratadine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Azelastine (Astelin) is the intranasal histamine-1 (H1) antagonist whose side effects are a bitter taste and epistaxis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which histamine receptor type increases postcapillary permeability, mucus secretion and bronchoconstriction?","choices":["Histamine-2 (H2) receptors","Histamine-3 (H3) receptors","Histamine-4 (H4) receptors","Histamine-1 (H1) receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Histamine-1 (H1) receptors raise postcapillary permeability and mucus secretion and cause bronchoconstriction. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which histamine receptor type increases GASTRIC ACID secretion and the force of heart contraction?","choices":["Histamine-2 (H2) receptors","Histamine-1 (H1) receptors","Histamine-3 (H3) receptors","Histamine-4 (H4) receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Histamine-2 (H2) receptors raise gastric acid and pepsin secretion and the force of heart contraction. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which class of drugs causes MAJOR SEDATION that is additive with alcohol?","choices":["Second-generation antihistamines","First-generation antihistamines","Nasal corticosteroids","Oral decongestants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-generation histamine-1 (H1) blockers cross into the central nervous system, so sedation is the major side effect. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which class of drugs HARDLY enters the central nervous system, so it causes much LESS SEDATION?","choices":["First-generation antihistamines","Nasal corticosteroids","Second-generation antihistamines","Oral decongestants"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Second-generation histamine-1 (H1) blockers largely stay out of the central nervous system, so they cause much less sedation. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which nasal corticosteroid is sold as FLONASE?","choices":["Mometasone","Triamcinolone","Beclomethasone","Fluticasone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fluticasone is sold as Flonase; Flovent is the inhaled asthma version, so the names must not be mixed up. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which nasal corticosteroid is sold as NASONEX?","choices":["Mometasone","Flunisolide","Budesonide","Triamcinolone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mometasone is the nasal corticosteroid sold as Nasonex. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which nasal corticosteroid is sold as BECONASE AQ?","choices":["Fluticasone","Beclomethasone","Budesonide","Flunisolide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beclomethasone is the nasal corticosteroid sold as Beconase AQ. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which class of drugs causes NOSEBLEEDS, septal perforation and an unpleasant taste?","choices":["Oral decongestants","Expectorants","Nasal corticosteroids","Antitussives"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nasal corticosteroids mostly cause local effects: epistaxis, septal perforation and an unpleasant taste. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Prednisone is converted in the liver to which active glucocorticoid, sold as the oral liquid Orapred?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Methylprednisolone","Hydrocortisone","Prednisolone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prednisolone (Orapred) is the active form of prednisone and is the liquid formulation, while prednisone (Deltasone) is the tablet. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which class causes hypokalemia, TENDON RUPTURE, cataracts and glaucoma?","choices":["Systemic corticosteroids","Nasal corticosteroids","Oral decongestants","Expectorants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Systemic steroids such as prednisone and dexamethasone cause low potassium, tendon rupture, cataracts, glaucoma and weakened immunity. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which class of drugs carries the counseling to avoid LIVE VACCINES and exposure to chickenpox and measles?","choices":["Nasal corticosteroids","Systemic corticosteroids","Oral decongestants","Antitussives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patient on a systemic steroid such as prednisone is immunocompromised at immunosuppressive doses, so live vaccines and exposure to chickenpox and measles are avoided. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which nasal spray causes REBOUND RHINITIS (rhinitis medicamentosa) if used for more than a few days?","choices":["Azelastine","Mometasone","Oxymetazoline","Pseudoephedrine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oxymetazoline (Afrin) causes rebound rhinitis (rhinitis medicamentosa) if used for more than 3 to 5 days. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which nasal spray interacts with MONOAMINE OXIDASE INHIBITORS and antidepressants?","choices":["Azelastine","Mometasone","Fluticasone","Oxymetazoline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oxymetazoline's listed interactions are with monoamine oxidase inhibitors and antidepressants. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which drug can cause TACHYCARDIA, hypertension and headache, and weaken the effect of blood pressure medicines?","choices":["Pseudoephedrine","Benzonatate","Guaifenesin","Dextromethorphan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pseudoephedrine (Sudafed) is an oral sympathomimetic decongestant; it can cause tachycardia and hypertension and decreases the effect of antihypertensives. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which antitussive ANESTHETIZES the stretch receptors in the lungs?","choices":["Dextromethorphan","Benzonatate","Guaifenesin","N-acetylcysteine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate (Tessalon) anesthetizes the stretch receptors in the lungs to relieve a non-productive cough. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which cough drug is contraindicated with an allergy to TETRACAINE or related products?","choices":["Dextromethorphan","Guaifenesin","Benzonatate","N-acetylcysteine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate is contraindicated in allergy to the product or related products such as tetracaine. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which antitussive numbs the mouth if its capsule is CHEWED?","choices":["Dextromethorphan","Codeine","Guaifenesin","Benzonatate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Benzonatate is swallowed whole; chewing the capsule causes local anesthesia of the mouth. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which antitussive is related to codeine and suppresses the cough center in the medulla through sigma receptor activation?","choices":["Dextromethorphan","Benzonatate","Guaifenesin","Dornase alfa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dextromethorphan (Robitussin, Delsym) is related to codeine and suppresses the medullary cough center through sigma receptors. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which cough drug is contraindicated within 2 weeks of taking a MONOAMINE OXIDASE INHIBITOR?","choices":["Benzonatate","Dextromethorphan","Guaifenesin","N-acetylcysteine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dextromethorphan raises the risk of serotonin syndrome, so it is contraindicated during or within 2 weeks of a monoamine oxidase inhibitor. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which drug's counseling is to drink plenty of fluid and expect INCREASED DRAINAGE?","choices":["Dextromethorphan","Benzonatate","Guaifenesin","Dornase alfa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Guaifenesin loosens mucus and decreases its viscosity, so patients drink plenty of fluid and expect more drainage. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 60"}]};
